WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.969
 

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.746
At first, it's very bright.

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:15.982
But then, as your eyes adjust to the light,

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.486
you start to piece together
your surroundings.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:22.288
The moving blobs above you, you realize,

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.657
are masked faces.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:28.928
And they've got their
hands inside your body.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.131
Okay, who messed up?

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.699
You can't run,

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.167
you can't scream,

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.369
luckily, you can't feel.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.372
But still, you have to watch...

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.174
This is 'What If,'

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:45.645
and here's what happens
if you wake up during surgery.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:51.317
The odds of you waking up
during surgery are about 1 in 19,000.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.286
That might not sound like a lot but,

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:54.954
considering that doctors perform

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:58.558
over 130,000 inpatient surgeries
daily in the U.S. alone,

00:00:58.558 --> 00:00:59.893
over 130,000 inpatient surgeries
daily in the U.S. alone,

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:03.063
it's close to 7 people every day.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:08.768
When it does happen, it's usually painless,
and you're usually back under in 5 minutes.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:12.105
But that's not to say there
haven't been some horror stories.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.775
In a study of people who've
experienced anesthesia awareness,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:18.711
18% reported feeling pain,

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.281
and some even developed
post traumatic symptoms

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:25.852
like increased stress,
nightmares, and flashbacks.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:36.162
Before any surgery, an anesthesiologist
will review your medical history,

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.965
and ask you about your habits
and your lifestyle.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.000
Everyone reacts differently to anesthetics,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.736
so, it's the anesthesiologist's job

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.906
to figure out a dose that will keep
you unconsious during surgery,

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:50.243
without inhibiting any of
your critical life functions.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:52.078
As you're counting down,

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:55.815
getting heavier, getting sleepier, getting...

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:56.850
Up soon?

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.187
What? Is it morning already?

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:02.755
Where am I?

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.929
Anesthesia alertness can 
play out in different ways.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:14.934
It usually involves complications
with the sedative, the paralytic, or both.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:18.037
If the paralytic wears off,
but the sedative doesn't.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.674
You'll remain unconsious,
but your body might still move.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.577
Anesthetists are normally
quick to pick up on this,

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:28.581
and will immediately fix the problem
by adding more paralytic agent.

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:33.119
Then there's the problem of both the
sedative and the paralytic wearing off.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:37.657
This is also easy to catch, because
the patient might try to speak or sit up.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:41.828
But again, doctors will have you
back under in less than five minutes.

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.530
Perhaps the most traumatic outcome

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:47.267
is if the sedative wears off,
but the paralytic doesn't.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:51.437
In this scenario, you can wake up
during surgery, and you might even feel it.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.607
But with the paralytic agent
working as it's supposed to,

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.210
you can't communicate that you're awake.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.579
While that scenario is particularly rare,

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:03.583
it's happened enough times to
inspire one too many movie ideas.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.818
But, if you're scared it could happen to you,

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:10.190
there are ways to further reduce
your risk of anesthesia alertness.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.193
It's always important have
up-to-date medical records,

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:17.230
and to follow your doctor's instructions
carefully before surgery.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.466
For bonus points, you can cut back
on alcohol and tobacco,

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:25.939
since, heavy drinking and smoking can affect
how your body responds to anesthetic drugs.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.174
As long as you keep
making healthy choices,

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.410
and don't let the movies
scare you too much,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.413
then your next surgery
should go smoothly.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:34.414
When you wake up

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.648
at the right time

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.417
you can thank your doctor;

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:41.221
and in your downtime, 
you can thank, 'What If.'

